p('Exact stopping distance six in a QC-GHP cyclic-cover family','Title2')
p('24 October 2026 · Verified checkpoint · Follow-up to L-F16-stopping-bound','Small2')
p('<b>Abstract.</b> For every positive integer t, we give explicit binary CSS check matrices of length 882t whose X-check graph has stopping distance exactly six. The matrices use lift length 63t and the fixed polynomials displayed below. A sound degree-one extension search, reduced to two roots and evaluated in Lean’s kernel, proves the base lower bound; a checked local-cover argument transfers it to every member of the family. A companion Z-check row gives the upper witness. We also prove that the actual expanded X-matrix has second squared singular value at least 12. Consequently the source spectral condition fails, and both Zhu–Chugg two-eigenvalue expressions are negative whenever their strict gap holds. All new proofs and source-to-matrix bridges have checked Lean versions.')
h('1. Question, source and precise answer')
p('Abiad and Castriota [1], 15 July 2026, arXiv:2607.13666v1, Proposition 5.1, Remark 5.2 and Section 6, ask for QC-GHP parameters making their spectral stopping bound informative and for tighter bounds. The preceding research block establishes a scalar-factor obstruction to the first route. This paper answers our narrower, incidence-aware follow-up: can actual incidences certify the exact stopping distance of the running B1 matrix and its positive 63-multiple covers?')
p('<b>Theorem 1.</b> Let t be a positive integer, ℓ=63t, and let P(s) have entries P(s)<sub>i,j</sub>=1 exactly when i=j+s in Z/ℓ. Let S be the forward cyclic shift of order 7. Over F₂[x]/(x<super>ℓ</super>−1), put')
eq('A=x²⁷I₇+S+x⁵⁴S², &nbsp; b=1+x+x⁶,<br/>H<sub>X</sub>=[Ã | I₇⊗B̃ᵀ], &nbsp; H<sub>Z</sub>=[I₇⊗B̃ | Ãᵀ].')
p('Then H<sub>X</sub>H<sub>Z</sub>ᵀ=0 over F₂. Each matrix has 882t columns and 441t rows; H<sub>X</sub> has column weight 3 and row weight 6. Every nonempty H<sub>X</sub> stopping support has size at least six, and an H<sub>Z</sub> row is a size-six stopping support. Thus the H<sub>X</sub> stopping distance is six. For the real expanded matrix, σ₂(H<sub>X</sub>)²≥12, counting Gram eigenvalues in descending order with multiplicity.')
p('<b>Scope.</b> These are CSS check matrices. We do not establish logical dimension, logical distance, rate or decoding performance for the covers. A stabilizer support is not a logical operator. At t=1 the displayed matrices are the source’s Example 3.2; its reported [[882,24,d]], 18≤d≤24 parameters are cited, not reproved here. The family does not satisfy Proposition 5.1’s spectral inequality.')
page();h('2. A sound lower certificate on the base')
p('For a binary incidence relation E(v,c), a stopping support is a nonempty finite variable set T such that no check has exactly one neighbor in T. No elementary, connected-support, or even-syndrome hypothesis is imposed. With checks (j,f) in Z/7×Z/ℓ and variables (side,j,f), the source matrices give precisely these column lists:')
eq('left(j,f): (j,f+27), (j+1,f), (j+2,f+54);<br/>right(j,f): (j,f), (j,f−1), (j,f−6).')
p('<b>Lemma 2 (extension soundness).</b> Suppose S is a duplicate-free list contained in a stopping support T. If a check has one neighbor in S, it has a second neighbor in T outside S. Branching over every other neighbor of that check therefore preserves at least one branch contained in T. If |T|≤|S|+f, f further additions suffice to reach a partial set with no degree-one check. Consequently rejection of every branch excludes all such stopping supersets.')
p('<b>Proof.</b> If no check has degree one in S, the test accepts. Otherwise the stopping property supplies the extra neighbor. Append that neighbor, retain duplicate-freeness and containment, reduce the fuel by one, and apply induction. Completeness of the explicit row list and the check picker’s degree-one condition are proved separately. An accepting branch is not used to infer a lower bound. □')
p('<b>Lemma 3 (two roots).</b> Simultaneously translating both cyclic coordinates of all variables and checks preserves incidence, support cardinality, and the stopping property. Every selected variable translates to (side,0,0). Thus it suffices to exclude small stopping supports containing either of the two side representatives.')
p('<b>Proof.</b> Each entry in the displayed incidence lists is affine with coefficient one in the translated coordinates. Translation is a bijection on variables and on checks. Image cardinalities and the selected-neighbor cardinality at every check are therefore preserved. Nonemptiness supplies the selected variable. □')
p('<b>Base certificate.</b> At ℓ=63 the checked Boolean extension test rejects both roots with four further additions. Lemmas 2–3 exclude every stopping support of size at most five. This is an exact finite certificate, evaluated by <font name="Bold">decide +kernel</font>; neither external enumeration nor native evaluation is a proof premise.')
p('The independent Python reconstruction also searched all 882 base roots, using 688,842 search nodes, without finding a support of size at most five. This corroborates the model and implementation. The Lean certificate and its soundness theorem supply the proof.')
eq('normalized_roots_reject :<br/>∀ b : Bool, survivesList pickList row 4 [(b,0,0)] = false<br/><br/>running_lower_six (T : Finset Var) (hT : Stopping E T) : 6 ≤ T.card')
p('The search strategy is classical rooted narrowing and branch-and-bound; Wang–Kulkarni–Poor [3] are relevant prior art. The contribution here is the checked instance, exact source translation and cover extension, rather than a new general enumeration algorithm.')
page();h('3. Cover transfer, upper witness and CSS bridge')
p('<b>Lemma 4 (local-cover transfer).</b> Let variable and check maps take a graph to a base graph. Assume: incidences map to incidences; every base check incident to the image of a selected variable has an incident lift at that variable; and the variable map is injective on each check’s neighbor set. Then the image of every stopping support is a stopping support, with cardinality at most the original support.')
p('<b>Proof.</b> The image is nonempty. If a base check meets it, choose a selected variable over that meeting and lift the check. The original stopping property provides a different selected neighbor at the lifted check. Local injectivity makes their images distinct; incidence preservation puts both at the base check. Hence the base check cannot have selected degree one. The finite-image cardinality inequality gives the size claim. □')
p('For ℓ=63t, reduction Z/ℓ→Z/63 maps both explicit column lists onto their base lists. The six neighbors of any lifted check map to six distinct base neighbors. The reduction homomorphism, incidence preservation, lifting and local injectivity are all proved in Lean. Lemma 4 and the base lower certificate give the lower bound six for every positive t; this is not extrapolation from sampled lift lengths.')
p('<b>Upper witness.</b> For every such lift the following support has six distinct variables:')
eq('left(0,0), left(0,−1), left(0,−6),<br/>right(0,27), right(1,0), right(2,54).')
p('Every incidence of a selected variable has an explicitly checked selected partner on the other side, so no check has selected degree one. The support is exactly H<sub>Z</sub> row (0,0). For the base, kernel reduction checks that every induced check degree is zero or two. These facts give the upper bound six. Combining it with the lower bound proves Theorem 1’s exact stopping distance.')
p('<b>Actual matrix bridge.</b> The formalization first defines the displayed matrices over the naturals. Duplicate-free column lists imply their entries are zero or one, and their count predicates are proved equivalent to the incidence lists. Casting these entries to F₂ preserves precisely the same graph. Separate counting lemmas prove the dimensions and H<sub>X</sub> degrees. Thus the stopping theorem concerns the actual binary matrices, not only an abstract graph model.')
p('<b>CSS proof.</b> On the finite abelian product Z/7×Z/ℓ, Ã and D=I₇⊗B̃ are circulant matrices. Circulant commutation gives ÃDᵀ=DᵀÃ. The expanded binary product is ÃDᵀ+DᵀÃ, which vanishes in characteristic two. All identities, transpose conventions and casts are checked for the displayed matrices.')
p('The existence of stabilizer stopping supports is known in quantum erasure decoding [4,5], with the mechanism attributed there to Connolly et al. [6]. The witness is an instance of that mechanism. The exact lower bound and the cover comparison are separate claims; the witness alone does not establish them.')
page();h('4. Spectral comparison and known stopping sets')
p('<b>Theorem 5.</b> For every positive lift in Theorem 1, the actual real matrix has σ₂²≥12.')
p('<b>Proof.</b> Consider the two-dimensional plane of row vectors constant on fibers, supported at block rows 0 and 3, with real coefficients a and b. Its squared norm is ℓ(a²+b²). Direct sums over the expanded matrix show that the left block contributes 3ℓ(a²+b²) and the right block contributes 9ℓ(a²+b²). The energy is therefore 12 times the squared norm. The linear functional giving the top eigenvector coefficient has a nonzero kernel on this plane. A nonzero vector in that kernel has Rayleigh quotient at most the second Gram eigenvalue, including multiplicity. Hence that eigenvalue is at least 12. All finite sums and the spectral reduction are checked in Lean. □')
p('Proposition 5.1 of [1] requires σ₂²&lt;γ/2; here γ=3 and 12&gt;3/2, so the condition fails. In the preceding block, inspection of the exact radicals and the separate exclusion of the small-size branch also showed why satisfying the spectral inequality alone need not give a useful stopping-distance guarantee. We do not apply that proposition outside its hypotheses.')
p('For comparison, Zhu–Chugg [2], Corollary 6, equations (41)–(42), give the following two regular-graph stopping bounds, with N variables, column degree γ, row degree k and second Gram eigenvalue η:')
eq('N(2γ−η)/(γk−η),<br/>2N(2γ+k−2−η)/(k(γk−η)).')
p('Their strict spectral-gap regime is η&lt;γk, with the connected regular setting supplying the unique top eigenvalue. These formulas have no separate small-support branch. They use a different threshold from [1]. In our family N=14ℓ, γ=3, k=6 and η≥12. If η&lt;18, both denominators are positive but the numerators have factors 6−η and 10−η, both negative. Thus both expressions are negative. If the strict gap fails, the displayed bounds are outside that regime. The two cases require no numerically inferred connectivity or spectral gap. This comparison does not dismiss Zhu–Chugg’s broader multi-eigenvector or irregular analysis.')
p('As an illustration only, independent dense reconstruction at ℓ=63 gives η≈15.89294145, and the two formula values ≈−4141.1162 and ≈−822.2481. Floating-point values do not enter the proof.')
p('Prabhu et al. [4] distinguish stabilizer, horizontal and vertical stopping supports and study erasure decoders. Their constituent expansion conditions are not spectral conditions on expanded H<sub>X</sub>. Yao et al. [5] explicitly simulate B1 and study post-peeling cluster decoding. The inspected full texts do not supply the lower certificate proved here. Plain peeling obstructions and quantum-aware correctability are different questions.')
p('The 2026 HGP stopping-set paper [7] remains a full-text access gap; its abstract is insufficient for a complete theorem comparison. The public code and X searches were bounded; they support no global absence or priority claim. The detailed search and source-screening ledgers accompany the proof bundle.')
page();h('5. Formal evidence and reproducibility')
p('The aggregate theorem <b>Incidence.Family.positive_family</b> quantifies over every natural t with [NeZero t] and combines dimensions, H<sub>X</sub> degrees, actual binary CSS commutation, the universal lower bound for stopping supports and an existential size-six witness. The source B1 consumer specializes it to t=1. The spectral theorem uses the descending ordered Gram spectrum of the same expanded real matrix.')
